WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
This yeast cell lacks a gene called RAD9.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.600
Irradiate it, and it does not pause in G2.

3
00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:10.840
It keeps dividing for several generations, and dies.

4
00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:15.840
Hold it back with a drug for a few hours, and it repairs the damage.

5
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.180
What was the gene for?

6
00:00:21.183 --> 00:00:23.283
So repair could still happen.

7
00:00:24.483 --> 00:00:25.883
RAD9 was needed for waiting.

8
00:00:27.283 --> 00:00:30.523
Ted Weinert and Leland Hartwell found this in 1988.

9
00:00:31.817 --> 00:00:34.637
A year later, they named such controls checkpoints.

10
00:00:36.037 --> 00:00:39.057
A checkpoint makes one cell-cycle event wait for another.

11
00:00:40.457 --> 00:00:43.397
Without it, the cell divides on time, not on completion.

12
00:00:44.617 --> 00:00:46.857
Now run the logic the other way.

13
00:00:47.857 --> 00:00:51.337
What result would have said RAD9 is a repair gene after all?

14
00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:02.110
If the held cells had still failed to repair, that would point to repair, though not

15
00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:05.190
prove it. But more time rescued them.

16
00:01:06.183 --> 00:01:07.503
First, the sensor.

17
00:01:09.183 --> 00:01:11.903
Broken DNA ends call in a kinase, ATM.

18
00:01:13.203 --> 00:01:17.623
Single strands at a stalled replication fork call in ATR.

19
00:01:17.623 --> 00:01:21.843
ATM mainly switches on Chk2, and ATR, Chk1.

20
00:01:22.883 --> 00:01:27.903
In G2, these messengers inhibit the Cdc25 phosphatases.

21
00:01:29.263 --> 00:01:33.223
So Cdk1 keeps its inhibitory phosphates and stays off.

22
00:01:34.463 --> 00:01:35.463
The cell waits.

23
00:01:36.333 --> 00:01:41.313
In this branch, nothing was destroyed; the brake is a shift in a phosphate balance.

24
00:01:42.713 --> 00:01:46.573
Once the signal is silenced, a cell able to recover moves on.

25
00:01:47.617 --> 00:01:49.117
Here is the whole idea.

26
00:01:50.417 --> 00:01:52.617
A checkpoint is a brake wired to a sensor.

27
00:01:53.897 --> 00:01:58.537
While the sensor sees an unfinished job, the brake holds back the next transition.

28
00:01:59.857 --> 00:02:02.717
When the cell finally moves, the move is hard to undo.

29
00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:05.990
The same wiring guards anaphase.

30
00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:11.770
Each pair of sister chromatids should attach to opposite spindle poles, through

31
00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:13.350
structures called kinetochores.

32
00:02:14.433 --> 00:02:19.953
In flat rat-kangaroo cells, Conly Rieder's group found one chromosome attached to

33
00:02:19.953 --> 00:02:24.933
only one pole. They destroyed its unattached kinetochore with a laser.

34
00:02:26.313 --> 00:02:27.493
Does anaphase still wait?

35
00:02:33.533 --> 00:02:39.853
No. Anaphase followed about twenty minutes later, though that chromosome never

36
00:02:39.853 --> 00:02:40.853
reached the middle.

37
00:02:41.813 --> 00:02:44.673
The wait signal came from the unattached kinetochore.

38
00:02:45.667 --> 00:02:50.587
Unattached kinetochores promote assembly of the mitotic checkpoint complex.

39
00:02:51.667 --> 00:02:57.927
It holds back the APC/C, or anaphase-promoting complex, a ligase that marks proteins

40
00:02:57.927 --> 00:02:58.927
for destruction.

41
00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:01.730
The complex is a decoy key.

42
00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:07.050
It fills the slots on Cdc20 that would grip a real target.

43
00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:10.430
Securin and cyclin B are kept out.

44
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.860
When the last kinetochore attaches, the decoys stop coming and come apart.

45
00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:22.180
Then the APC/C tags securin and cyclin B, and the proteasome destroys them.

46
00:03:23.183 --> 00:03:29.263
Securin had held separase, a protease, inactive; in vertebrates, so had Cdk1.

47
00:03:30.543 --> 00:03:35.563
Freed separase cuts the cohesin holding sister chromatids together, and anaphase

48
00:03:35.563 --> 00:03:41.223
begins. Losing cyclin B is a major way Cdk1 turns off.

49
00:03:42.233 --> 00:03:43.893
Why does it not run backwards?

50
00:03:45.233 --> 00:03:49.833
A phosphate can be put back within seconds to minutes, because the protein is still

51
00:03:49.833 --> 00:03:53.613
there. A destroyed protein must be made again.

52
00:03:54.633 --> 00:04:00.193
Potapova and colleagues forced cells out of mitosis with a Cdk1 inhibitor.

53
00:04:01.713 --> 00:04:02.713
Cyclin B was kept.

54
00:04:03.973 --> 00:04:06.473
After washout, many went back into mitosis.

55
00:04:08.173 --> 00:04:10.353
Cells whose cyclin B was destroyed did not.

56
00:04:11.383 --> 00:04:14.423
Entry into mitosis also rests on feedback.

57
00:04:16.143 --> 00:04:20.803
Active Cdk1 switches on its activator and switches off its inhibitor.

58
00:04:22.143 --> 00:04:24.163
Once the rise starts, it feeds itself.

59
00:04:25.217 --> 00:04:27.557
Once on, this switch tends to stay on.

60
00:04:28.717 --> 00:04:33.297
That may be why the damage brake normally acts in G2, before the switch flips.

61
00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:35.800
The brake can leak, though.

62
00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:42.140
A cell held in mitosis can eventually slip out, because cyclin B is still slowly

63
00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:43.140
destroyed.

64
00:04:43.783 --> 00:04:48.343
So at each guarded step, a cell asks one question: is the job finished?

65
00:04:49.683 --> 00:04:51.963
Until it is, a sensor holds the brake.

66
00:04:53.243 --> 00:04:57.923
Then destruction or feedback carries it forward, and the step is hard to take back.
